# EonlineBazar Cursor Rules
# Mandatory guidelines for all Cursor Agent prompts

---

## Project Identity

This is EonlineBazar — a full-stack e-commerce platform.

**Stack:** Node.js + Express (backend), MongoDB/Mongoose (legacy) + PostgreSQL/Prisma/Neon (primary), Vanilla JS (frontend).

**Migration status:** Dual-write active (Mongo + PG). PostgreSQL is now the primary read source (all `READ_PG_*` flags = true).

---

## Mandatory Pre-Task Reading

Before starting ANY task (feature, fix, refactor, or doc update), ALWAYS read these files first:

1. `ARCHITECTURE.md` — project structure, patterns, dev setup
2. `REFACTOR_MAP.md` — file-level change log
3. `SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md` — feature completeness status
4. The relevant audit file for the area being changed (see Audit File Map below)

If any of these files do not exist, CREATE them before proceeding.

---

## Audit File Map

Every area of the codebase has a designated audit file.
When working in an area, read its audit file first, then update it after.

| Area | Audit File |
|------|-----------|
| Backend API routes & controllers | `SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md` (Backend section) |
| Database / migration | `DATABASE_MIGRATION_AUDIT.md` |
| Frontend admin panel | `docs/audit/ADMIN_PANEL_AUDIT.md` |
| Frontend customer-facing | `docs/audit/CUSTOMER_FRONTEND_AUDIT.md` |
| Authentication & security | `docs/audit/AUTH_SECURITY_AUDIT.md` |
| HRM (HR module) | `docs/audit/HRM_AUDIT.md` |
| Orders & checkout | `docs/audit/ORDERS_AUDIT.md` |
| Products & catalog | `docs/audit/PRODUCTS_AUDIT.md` |
| Payments & finance | `docs/audit/PAYMENTS_FINANCE_AUDIT.md` |
| CMS (banners, pages, navbar) | `docs/audit/CMS_AUDIT.md` |
| Marketing (newsletter, coupons, campaigns) | `docs/audit/MARKETING_AUDIT.md` |
| Chat microservice | `docs/audit/CHAT_AUDIT.md` |
| DevOps / deployment | `docs/audit/DEVOPS_AUDIT.md` |

**Rules:**

- If an audit file for the area does NOT exist: CREATE it first, then do the work, then fill it in.
- If it EXISTS: read it before starting, update it after finishing.
- NEVER create a new audit file outside `docs/audit/` except `DATABASE_MIGRATION_AUDIT.md` (root level).
- Legacy root audit files (`CHAT_AUDIT.md`, `PROFILE_AUDIT.md`, `AUDIT_REPORT.md`) are historical — new findings go to the mapped file above.

---

## Audit File Format (mandatory for all audit files)

Every audit file must follow this structure:

```markdown
# [AREA NAME] AUDIT — EonlineBazar

**Last updated:** YYYY-MM-DD
**Scope:** [what subsystem this file covers]
**Status:** ✅ COMPLETE | ⚠️ PARTIAL | ❌ MISSING

---

## File Inventory

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| path/to/file.js | brief description |

---

## Feature Checklist

- [x] Feature name — key file(s)
- [ ] Incomplete feature — notes

---

## Known Issues / Gaps

| Issue | Severity | Status | Notes |
|-------|----------|--------|-------|
| Description | High/Medium/Low | Open/Fixed | context |

---

## Dependencies & Integrations

- External services, env vars, cross-audit links

---

## Change Log

### [Change Title] — YYYY-MM-DD

- What changed; files touched; test status
```

When updating an audit file after work: bump **Last updated**, update **File Inventory**, adjust **Feature Checklist**, add **Change Log** entry, update **Status** if changed.

---

## Auto-Audit Rules (MANDATORY — no exceptions)

After completing ANY task (feature, fix, refactor, even a 1-line change):

**STEP 1 — Update the relevant area audit file:**

- Add entry under "Change Log" with today's date
- Update "File Inventory" table for any new/changed/deleted files
- Update "Feature Checklist" checkboxes
- Update "Last updated" date at top
- Update "Status" if it changed

**STEP 2 — Update REFACTOR_MAP.md:**

- Add every new file created
- Add every existing file modified
- Add every file deleted

**STEP 3 — Update SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md:**

- Add a dated section: `## [Feature Name] — YYYY-MM-DD`
- Mark checklist items as ✅ ⚠️ or ❌

**STEP 4 — Update README.md:**

- Update feature list if a new feature was added
- Update test count if tests changed
- Update "Last updated" date

**STEP 5 — If new files were created:** add them to `ARCHITECTURE.md` under the correct section.

These 5 steps happen IN THE SAME RESPONSE as the code change — never in a separate prompt.

---

## Database Rules (PostgreSQL primary)

- PostgreSQL/Prisma is now the PRIMARY database for all reads and writes.
- All `READ_PG_*` flags are ON in production.
- For any new model: create a Prisma schema entry + repository file + dual-write (Mongo still receives writes for safety until Phase 4).
- Never write new features that are Mongoose-only.
- New repository files go in: `backend/src/repositories/{model}Repository.js`
- Follow the pattern in: `backend/src/repositories/categoryRepository.js`

---

## Code Placement Rules

- Never add code to barrel files directly (except one-line imports).
- Never add styles to `admin.css`, `profile.css`, `style.css` — use the module css files.
- Backend routes in `routes/*.js` are sacred — never restructure.
- All onclick/interactive HTML functions must be exposed via `window.functionName = fn`.
- Never change function names or IDs without searching all files first.

---

## Testing Rules

- Run `npm test` after every code change.
- Repository tests run with: `node --test --test-concurrency=1 tests/repositories/{file}.test.js`
- All tests must pass before marking a task complete.
- New repository = new test file required.

---

## New Feature Checklist (for any new feature)

When adding a completely new feature, do ALL of the following:

1. [ ] Read `ARCHITECTURE.md` + relevant audit file
2. [ ] Create or update Prisma schema (if new data model)
3. [ ] Create repository file (`backend/src/repositories/`)
4. [ ] Create repository test file (`tests/repositories/`)
5. [ ] Create or update controller (`backend/src/controllers/`)
6. [ ] Create or update route (`backend/src/routes/`)
7. [ ] Create or update frontend module (frontend JS/CSS files)
8. [ ] Wire dual-write (Mongo + PG)
9. [ ] Run `npm test` — all pass
10. [ ] Update all 5 audit targets (area audit + REFACTOR_MAP + SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT + README + ARCHITECTURE if needed)

---

## Commit Message Format

```
git commit -m "type(area): short description

- bullet: what changed
- bullet: files affected"
```

**Types:** feat / fix / refactor / docs / test / chore

**Examples:**

- `feat(products): add bulk price update endpoint`
- `fix(orders): correct status transition validation`
- `docs(audit): update PRODUCTS_AUDIT.md after variant refactor`

---

## After Every Task — Final Checklist

Before declaring a task done:

- [ ] Code works and is tested
- [ ] Relevant audit file updated
- [ ] REFACTOR_MAP.md updated
- [ ] SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md updated
- [ ] README.md updated
- [ ] npm test passes
- [ ] List of modified files shown to user

---

## Documentation

- **Documentation Autonomy:** When implementing any new feature, fix, or refactor, you are fully authorized and required to update docs (`README.md`, `SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md`, `REFACTOR_MAP.md`, area audit files, etc.) as needed.
- **Automated README Maintenance:** After completing any coding task, automatically update `README.md` in the same flow. Do NOT wait for a separate prompt.
- **No Source Code Disturbance on Doc Tasks:** Documentation-only work must not touch `.js`, `.html`, `.css` unless the user explicitly asks for code.
- Operational and env notes belong in `ARCHITECTURE.md` (Local Development Quick Reference) — `ADMIN_NOTES.md` is intentionally blank.

---

## Permission System Rules

1. Every new sidebar item MUST have an entry in `SECTION_PERMISSIONS` (`permissions.js`).

2. Every new API route MUST use `checkPermission()` with the most specific key first.

3. When adding a new feature section:
   a. Add permission key(s) to `PERMISSIONS` array
   b. Add sidebar mapping to `SECTION_PERMISSIONS`
   c. Add route guard with `checkPermission()`
   d. Add to `PERMISSION_IMPLICATIONS` if it is a child of a coarse key
   e. Update `HRM_AUDIT.md` (or the relevant area audit file)

4. Never use role-based checks (`req.account.role`) in route guards — always use `checkPermission()`.

5. Frontend tabs within a section MUST use `data-permission="key"` attribute to show/hide.

6. Each sidebar item must have its own unique permission key. Never map 2+ sidebar items to the same coarse key (e.g. do not reuse `manage_orders` for POS, abandoned carts, and orders).

7. Coarse permission keys (`manage_orders`, `manage_inventory`, `manage_marketing`, `manage_catalog`, etc.) must be marked `preset_only: true` and never appear in permission modal toggles. All sidebar items use granular keys only. `getPermissionCatalog()` filters out `preset_only` entries.

---

## Production Checklist

Before any PR merge:

1. `npm test` must pass (231/231)
2. New routes must have `checkPermission()`
3. New sidebar items must have `data-permission` attribute
4. New permission keys must be added to `PERMISSIONS` array with correct group
5. Dual-write: both Mongo AND PG saves must be implemented
6. No debug `console.log` in production code
7. Update `HRM_AUDIT.md` for HRM changes
8. Update `MASTER_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md`

---

## When in doubt — which `.md` file?

| Topic | File |
|-------|------|
| Enterprise / cross-cutting backend status | `SYSTEM_ENTERPRISE_AUDIT.md` |
| Domain-specific status | `docs/audit/[AREA]_AUDIT.md` |
| MongoDB → PostgreSQL migration | `DATABASE_MIGRATION_AUDIT.md` |
| Folder structure / dev setup | `ARCHITECTURE.md` |
| File-level change log | `REFACTOR_MAP.md` |





